# Exact invalid talent cleanup candidate

**Not applied.** Policy: `tc-invalid-talent-rank-v1`. Migration approval remains
**UNREVIEWED**. This package selects exactly **@COUNT@** audited
`TALENT_VALUE_IS_NOT_RANK_SPELL` rows across **@GUID_COUNT@** characters.
Keep these private files with the original audit, backup and execution reports.

Source: `@SOURCE@`, server UUID `@UUID@`.
New archive: `@ARCHIVE@`. Batch key: `@BATCH@`.

Only the exact `(guid,spell,talentGroup)` keys in `selected-rows.tsv` can be
removed. Each value was independently checked against the matching TC
Talent.dbc rank spell catalog. A number matching a TalentEntry ID is never
converted into a guessed rank. Valid rank spells remain, including those with
separate class/group conflicts. Disabled/unowned spell history, ordinary and
dependent spell ranks, character_spell, skills, glyphs and items are untouched.
Orphan talent rows are a separate category and are not selected implicitly.

## Review and prepare

Use only the stopped restored preparation copy, never production or an existing
migration target. Preserve the untouched baseline and verify your backup.
Keep every core and other writer stopped. The acknowledgement below is an
operator assertion, not automatic detection that all services are stopped.
The UUID and audited schema must still match. Keep the exact audited DBC assets
and toolkit frozen; a changed catalog requires a new audit/candidate.

`00-setup.sql` creates only the new archive. Existing archive names are refused.
Do not use --force or interactive SOURCE; use fresh batch clients. If setup
fails, inspect the partial new archive instead of silently dropping/reusing it.
DDL is separate from the transactional actions.

Setup needs an administrator with CREATE/CREATE ROUTINE on the new archive and
direct global SELECT/TRIGGER privileges for complete metadata visibility. A
parameterless read-only metadata guard uses that administrator as definer;
other procedures use invoker security. Keep the setup account available.
Preparation needs SELECT on the two source tables and archive DML/EXECUTE.
Apply additionally needs DELETE only on source character_talent; restore needs
INSERT only on that table. No source character, spell, skill, item, auth or
world writes are needed. The restricted writer needs no source TRIGGER grant.

Replace the private option-file path with your preparation connection:

```bash
sha256sum --check SHA256SUMS
mysql --defaults-extra-file=/secure/path/preparation-client.cnf --skip-force \
  < 00-setup.sql
mysql --defaults-extra-file=/secure/path/preparation-client.cnf --skip-force \
  --init-command="SET @talent_cleanup_ack='STOPPED_PREPARATION_COPY_ONLY'" \
  < 01-prepare.sql
mysql --defaults-extra-file=/secure/path/preparation-client.cnf --skip-force \
  < 04-status.sql > prepared-status.private.tsv
```

Preparation writes only to the archive. In one SERIALIZABLE transaction it
captures complete original character rows, all talent rows for the affected
characters, and the exact @COUNT@ rows to remove. Inspect the status output,
manifest and selected keys before deciding to apply. Full snapshots are in
`character_before`, `talent_before` and `talent_removed` in the archive.

The earlier audit digest establishes provenance, not a hash of every database
column. Preparation rechecks the exact invalid keys, current invalid set and
audited total talent count (@SOURCE_COUNT@), then snapshots current full affected
state. Apply/restore reject later same-count changes to those talent sets or
any affected character field. Changes to valid talents before preparation are
not proven absent by the old audit; review the prepared current state.

Native InnoDB tables, matching audited columns and native three-column talent
representation are required. Schema/catalog/manifest drift, generated/invisible
source columns, incomplete metadata visibility, source triggers, talent foreign
keys, missing/online affected characters and unexpected counts are blockers.
Do not widen predicates or change archive data to bypass a blocker.

Ordinary expression defaults (`EXTRA=DEFAULT_GENERATED`), including timestamps
with `DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`, are supported. Their stored values, including
historical timestamps and NULLs, are copied explicitly into the archive.
Computed virtual/stored columns and invisible columns remain unsupported.
A failed prepare rolls back its snapshots and makes no source changes.

## Apply after reviewing this batch

```bash
mysql --defaults-extra-file=/secure/path/preparation-client.cnf --skip-force \
  --init-command="SET @talent_cleanup_ack='STOPPED_PREPARATION_COPY_ONLY'" \
  < 02-apply.sql
mysql --defaults-extra-file=/secure/path/preparation-client.cnf --skip-force \
  < 04-status.sql > applied-status.private.tsv
```

Apply removes only @COUNT@ exact archived rows, verifies retained talent
ownership and full character state, and marks APPLIED in the same transaction.
The resulting total talent count must be @AFTER_COUNT@. Errors/interruption
roll back incomplete changes. Resume by calling apply again: matching APPLIED
state is verified without deleting again or changing its timestamp. No ranks
are added and no talent points, specs, skills or profession slots are reset.

`04-status.sql` displays archived originals; their presence after apply is
expected. APPLIED means the procedure's checks and commit succeeded, not native
gameplay or production acceptance.

## Exact restoration before further preparation or gameplay

```bash
mysql --defaults-extra-file=/secure/path/preparation-client.cnf --skip-force \
  --init-command="SET @talent_cleanup_ack='STOPPED_PREPARATION_COPY_ONLY'" \
  < 03-restore.sql
```

Restore verifies the complete affected character/talent state and reinserts
only the @COUNT@ original archived rows. Changes and RESTORED state commit
together. Archives remain. A repeated restore verifies original state without
changing it; apply after restore is refused. Restoration intentionally
reinstates the original bad rows. Legitimate talent/spec/character changes,
online flags, missing rows, schema/catalog changes or changed counts cause
refusal; this is not a repair tool for data after gameplay.

After an approved apply, rerun the read-only talent audit into a fresh directory
and compare classifications. This reason should disappear for live characters;
orphan and other reason codes remain separate. Repeat the full audit after all
reviewed preparation batches. Never mass-delete disabled spells or reinterpret
talent IDs to make other migration blockers disappear. Some blockers require
migrator compatibility coverage rather than source cleanup.

Use a fresh migration with new target/control schemas after source edits;
never resume checkpoints captured before a cleanup. In isolated gameplay tests,
check talent points and both specs, casts/trainers, professions, recipes and
skills/caps, then save/relog and restart. Those acceptance gates remain
**NOT TESTED** by generating or applying this SQL. Release approval stays
**UNREVIEWED**.
